==English==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|turnen out}}, {{m|enm|tornen out}}, equivalent to {{af|en|turn|out|nocat=1}}.

The slang and prison terms meaning "to turn into a prostitute, etc." are probably an ellipsis for {{m|en||turn (inside) out|t=to flip someone's character or role}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{audio|en|En-au-turn out.ogg|a=AU}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb|*}}

# {{lb|en|intransitive|idiomatic|copulative}} To [[end up]]; to [[result#Verb|result]].
#: {{ux|en|I had hoped our first meeting would '''turn out''' better.}}
#: {{ux|en|The truth '''turned out''' to be more depressing than we thought.}}
#: {{ux|en|I couldn't have assisted anyway, as it '''turned out'''.}}
#* {{RQ:Churchill Celebrity|chapter=I|page=2|passage=He used to drop into my chambers once in a while to smoke, and was first-rate company. When I gave a dinner there was generally a cover laid for him. I liked the man for his own sake, and even had he promised to '''turn out''' a celebrity it would have had no weight with me.}}
#* {{RQ:Plato Tredennick Socrates|page=21|passage=I should like this to be the result, gentlemen, assuming it be for your advantage and my own; and I should like to be successful in my defence; but I think that it will be difficult, and I am quite aware of the nature of my task. However, let that '''turn out''' as God wills; I must obey the law and make my defence.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|by ellipsis}} To [[succeed]]; [[work out]]; turn out well.
#: {{ux|en|I'm afraid the cake didn't '''turn out'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|title=Diary|author={{w|Lou Sullivan}}|date=13 Feb 1965|text=Starting a new story, I hope it '''turns out'''.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|idiomatic}} To [[attend]]; [[show up]].
#: {{ux|en|Hundreds of people '''turned out''' to see the parade.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|dated}} To [[go out]]; to leave one's home.
#* {{RQ:Beckett Watt|text=But then one of Mr. Knott's men would have had to put on his coat and hat and '''turn out''', as likely as not in the pitch dark, and in torrents of rain in all probability, and grope his way in the dark in the pours of rain, with the pot of food in his hand, a wretched and ridiculous figure, to where the dog lay.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|idiomatic}} To [[extinguish]] a [[light]] or other device.
#: {{syn|en|turn off|switch out|switch off}}
#: {{ux|en|'''Turn out''' the lights before you leave.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|idiomatic}} To become [[apparent]] or known, especially ''(as) it turns out''
#: {{ux|en|It '''turns out''' that he just made a lucky guess.}}
#* {{RQ:Allingham China Governess|chapter=19|passage=As soon as Julia returned with a constable, Timothy, who was on the point of exhaustion, prepared to give over to him gratefully. The newcomer '''turned out''' to be a powerful youngster, fully trained and eager to help, and he stripped off his tunic at once.}}
#* {{RQ:Guardian|date=September 15, 2012|author=Amy Lawrence
|title=Arsenal's Gervinho enjoys the joy of six against lowly Southampton
|url=http://www.guardian.co.uk/football/2012/sep/15/arsenal-southampton-premier-league
|passage=The Ivorian is a player with such a liking for improvisation it does not usually look like he has any more idea than anyone else what he is going to do next, so it was an interesting choice. As it '''turned out''', it was a masterstroke. The striker was full of running, played with a more direct shoot-on-sight approach than normal and finished with two goals and an assist.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=May14, 2024|author=Clay Drinko|work=Psychology Today
|title=[https://www.psychologytoday.com/gb/blog/play-your-way-sane/202405/lower-your-expectations Lower Your Expectations]
|passage=It '''turns out''', there’s research that backs up the usefulness of lowering our expectations. Participants in one study had to make decisions that either led to financial gains or losses, all while inside a magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) machine. Researchers then duplicated this experiment as an app to crowdsource more data.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|idiomatic}} To [[produce]]; make.
#: {{ux|en|The bakery '''turns out''' three hundred pies each day.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[leave]] a [[road]].
#: {{ux|en|'''Turn out''' at the third driveway.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[remove]] from a [[mould]], [[bowl]] etc.
#: {{ux|en|'''Turn out''' the dough onto a board and shape it.}}
#* {{RQ:Marsh Beetle|passage=He had found the pocket, and was '''turning out''' the contents.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}}  To [[empty]] for inspection.
#: {{ux|en|Please '''turn out''' your pockets.}}
#: {{ux|en|The security guard asked everyone to '''turn out''' their bags.}}
# {{senseid|en|refuse, eject, evict}} {{lb|en|transitive|idiomatic}} To [[refuse]] service or shelter; to [[eject]] or [[evict]].
#: {{cot|en|turn away|turn down}}
#: {{ux|en|The hotel staff hastened to '''turn out''' the noisy drunk.}}
#: {{ux|en|The poor family were '''turned out''' of their lodgings at only an hour's notice.}}
#: {{ux|en|The whole lot of grafters was later '''turned out''' of office.}}
#* {{RQ:Plato Tredennick Socrates|page=45|passage=A fine life I should have if I left this country at my age and spent the rest of my days trying one city after another and being '''turned out''' every time!}}
#* {{quote-book|en|title=Collins Bedtime Treasury of Nursery Rhymes and Tales|year=1998|section=Mary Had a Little Lamb|author=Jonathan Langley|page=39|passage=And so the teacher '''turned''' it '''out'''<br />But still it lingered near,<br />And waited patiently about<br />Till Mary did appear.}}
# {{lb|en|sex|transitive|slang}} To convince a person (usually a woman) to become a [[prostitute]]; to [[pimp]] or manage a prostitute.
#: {{syn|en|harlotize|whore out|Thesaurus:pimp out}}
